/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Every fiber link has three parts: a transmitter, the fiber channel and a receiver.
   - The fiber is a thin strand of silica glass.
   - The receiver uses a photodetector, usually a PIN photodiode or an avalanche photodiode (APD), to turn the arriving light into a current.
   - Copper cable carries the signal as an electric current.
   - Fiber also has drawbacks an examiner may ask for: splicing and connectors need precision tools, the glass breaks if bent too tightly, and the ends need E/O and O/E converters.
   - In an analog link the light power follows the input waveform continuously; radio-over-fiber feeds and older cable TV networks work this way.
   - Links are named by their wavelength window. 850 nm serves short multimode links inside buildings with cheap sources. 1310 nm is where pulse spreading in standard single-mode fiber is smallest, so it suits short and medium single-mode links.
   - Loss in a fiber is quoted in decibels per kilometre, and the total loss in dB grows in proportion to length:
   - α is the attenuation in dB/km and L is the fiber length in km.
   - P is the optical power in mW; 0 dBm means exactly 1 mW.
*/
