/* book: lead: Ray theory treats light in a fiber as straight lines that bend at boundaries by Snell's law. The core has a higher refractive index than the cladding, and a ray that meets the core–cladding boundary at a steep enough angle is reflected back completely: total internal reflection (TIR). Working backwards from that condition to the end face gives the acceptance angle, the half-angle of the cone of li */
