/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The refractive index n of a material is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum c to its speed v in the material: n = c/v.
   - At a boundary, Snell's law links the angles on the two sides: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂.
   - Going from the denser core into the rarer cladding, the ray bends away from the normal, so θ₂ > θ₁.
   - θc is measured from the normal at the core–cladding boundary; it exists only when n₁ > n₂.
   - For any angle of incidence above θc, Snell's law would need sin θ₂ > 1, which no real angle gives, so no light is transmitted and all of it is reflected back into the core.
   - Take a meridional ray, one that passes through the fiber axis.
   - The ray then meets the core–cladding wall.
   - The acceptance angle θa is the largest outside angle θ₀ that still meets this, so at the limit θr = 90° − θc:
   - θa is the acceptance half-angle in the outside medium, measured from the fiber axis.
   - Rotating θa about the axis sweeps out the acceptance cone.
*/
