/* book exam traps: Use sin θc = n₂/n₁, not n₁/n₂; with n₂ < n₁ the ratio is below 1, as a sine must be. | Angles in Snell's law are measured from the normal. A ray 15° from the wall is 75° from the wall's normal. | θc is inside the fiber at the core–cladding wall; θa is outside, at the end face, measured from the axis. Do not call θc the acceptance angle. | With a medium other than air at the end face, divide by n₀: sin θa = √(n₁² − n₂²)/n₀. The cone in water is narrower than in air. | A ray launched at a larger angle hits the wall at a smaller angle of incidence (φ = 90° − θr), so it is the steep launch that escapes, not the shallow one. */
